.TH DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER \- declare buffer for memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(name, elem_count, elem_size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a uint8_t array sized correctly for a memory pool with the
specified parameters. The buffer can then be passed to
mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). Automatically includes header
overhead in size calculation.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name        Variable name for the buffer
elem_count  Number of elements the pool will hold
elem_size   Size of each element in bytes
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Buffer is allocated on stack or in static storage depending on context
Useful for embedded systems or avoiding heap allocation
Buffer is declared with the alignment the pool's internal per-element
  header (a size_t plus a pointer) requires, so it can be handed
  directly to mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() with no
  further alignment considerations
elem_size smaller than sizeof(uintptr_t) is rounded up to fit the
  free-list pointer before the buffer's size is computed, the same
  way mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() itself rounds it, so
  the declared buffer always holds exactly elem_count elements once
  passed to that constructor with the same elem_size
The resulting per-element stride is further rounded up to the pool's
  internal per-element header's own alignment requirement, again
  matching mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() exactly, so every
  element in the resulting pool is correctly aligned, not just the first
elem_count must be nonzero; rejected at compile time (via a
  _Static_assert) if elem_count is zero, or if elem_count times the
  rounded and aligned elem_size would overflow size_t
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_MEMPOOL_BUFFER(my_buffer, 100, 64);
mempool *mp = mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    my_buffer, sizeof(my_buffer), 64, false, false, NULL, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer (3)
